course: dictionary
lesson: 1750
title: Sorting things into groups
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Classification means sorting things into groups because they share something. Scientists use it to put animals into groups such as mammals, birds and fish. A classification can also be the group something is put into, such as «mammal».
explain: To classify something, look for what the things have in common. A whale and a bat are both mammals, even though one lives in the sea and one flies, because both feed their babies milk. Sorting by what things share is the key idea.
explain: Classification can also mean an official level. «In the UK the British Board of Film Classification (BBFC) rates films: U, PG, 12A, 12, 15 and 18.» Each rating puts a film into a group that tells you who may watch it.
fact: Classification means sorting things into groups by what they have in common.
fact: A classification is also the group something is put into, such as «mammal».
fact: Scientists use classification to sort living things into vertebrates and invertebrates, and into mammals, birds, reptiles, fish and amphibians.
fact: In maths, shapes are classified by their sides, and numbers are classified as odd, even or prime.
fact: A film classification is a formal, official level: «15» and «18» mean nobody younger may buy or see the film.
exercise: Sorting animals into mammals, birds and fish is an example of what? | Exaggeration | Measurement | *Classification | Decoration
exercise: «In the UK the British Board of Film Classification rates films: U, PG, 12A, 12, 15 and 18.» What is a film rating an example of? | A secret password | A type of weather | *A formal classification
exercise: Odd and even are two groups used for numbers. What does sorting numbers into these groups do? | *Classifies them | Spells them | Multiplies them | Rounds them
test: «Nobody younger may buy or see a film rated 18.» What does this tell you about the 18 classification? | It is the cheapest film | It means the film is in colour | It is a type of camera | *It is an official level that sets who may watch it
test: Which example shows a system of classification? | A shopping list | A song with a catchy tune | A pile of mixed socks | *Sorting birds, fish and mammals into separate groups
exam: Which meaning fits classification best? | Breaking things into small bits | Losing things on purpose | *Sorting things into groups by what they have in common | Making things louder
